\nTherefore, the function $f(x)={log}_{e}⁡(4+2x)$ is continuous on the interval $(-2,\\infty ).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979915/state.json" "file_sha256" "1076857060cce611119c9949e654b80298580fb2702983314467b1d701db7eeb"} {"question" "q-340062" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979915/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "25cc545ce118b199073ded6344ed054eabd863cfb32a7fbe61dfc540a594989a"} {"question" "q-340062" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979915/state.json" "file_sha256" "1076857060cce611119c9949e654b80298580fb2702983314467b1d701db7eeb"} {"question" "q-340062" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979915/state.json" "file_sha256" "1076857060cce611119c9949e654b80298580fb2702983314467b1d701db7eeb"} {"question" "q-340062" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979915/state.json" "file_sha256" "1076857060cce611119c9949e654b80298580fb2702983314467b1d701db7eeb"} {"question" "q-340062" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\infty"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979915/state.json" "file_sha256" "1076857060cce611119c9949e654b80298580fb2702983314467b1d701db7eeb"} {"question" "q-40084" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The interval $I$ consists of all real numbers $x$ such that $-2\\pi \\le x\\le 0.$ What is the largest subset of $I$ on which the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)$ is continuous?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979915/state.json" "file_sha256" "1076857060cce611119c9949e654b80298580fb2702983314467b1d701db7eeb"} {"question" "q-40084" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Trigonometric functions are continuous at all points in their respective domains.\n\nThe function $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is continuous over the entire interval $[-2\\pi ,0],$ but the function $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ is not defined at $x=-2\\pi ,-\\pi ,0.$ \n\nTherefore, the domain of $f(x)$ consists of the union of the intervals $(-2\\pi ,-\\pi )$ and $(-\\pi ,0),$ namely\n\n$$\n(-2\\pi ,-\\pi )\\cup (-\\pi ,0)\n$$\n\nand the function is also continuous on this union of intervals." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979915/state.json" "file_sha256" "1076857060cce611119c9949e654b80298580fb2702983314467b1d701db7eeb"} {"question" "q-40084" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979915/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "25cc545ce118b199073ded6344ed054eabd863cfb32a7fbe61dfc540a594989a"} {"question" "q-40084" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979915/state.json" "file_sha256" "1076857060cce611119c9949e654b80298580fb2702983314467b1d701db7eeb"} {"question" "q-40084" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(-2\\pi ,-\\pi )\\cup (-\\pi ,0)"] ["math" "(-2\\pi ,-\\pi ]"] ["math" "[-2\\pi ,-\\pi )\\cup (-\\pi ,0]"] ["math" "[-2\\pi ,0)"] ["math" "[-\\pi ,0)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979915/state.json" "file_sha256" "1076857060cce611119c9949e654b80298580fb2702983314467b1d701db7eeb"} {"question" "q-40084" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(-2\\pi ,-\\pi )\\cup (-\\pi ,0)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979915/state.json" "file_sha256" "1076857060cce611119c9949e654b80298580fb2702983314467b1d701db7eeb"} {"question" "q-39765" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the largest union of intervals on which the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}-1})$ is continuous." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979915/state.json" "file_sha256" "1076857060cce611119c9949e654b80298580fb2702983314467b1d701db7eeb"} {"question" "q-39765" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function is a composition of two functions, a rational function, and a sine function.\n\nThe sine is continuous at all points in its domain, and its domain consists of all real numbers. So, we focus on the rational function.\n\nLikewise, the rational function $\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}-1}$ is continuous at all points in its domain. To find its domain, we start by factoring the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{(x-1)(x+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis function is continuous everywhere except at the excluded points $x=-1$ and $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the domain our original function consists of the union of the intervals $(-\\infty ,-1),$ $(-1,1),$ and $(1,\\infty ),$ namely\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (-1,1)\\cup (1,\\infty )\n$$\n\nand the function is also continuous on these intervals." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979915/state.json" "file_sha256" "1076857060cce611119c9949e654b80298580fb2702983314467b1d701db7eeb"} {"question" "q-39765" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979915/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "25cc545ce118b199073ded6344ed054eabd863cfb32a7fbe61dfc540a594989a"} {"question" "q-39765" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979915/state.json" "file_sha256" "1076857060cce611119c9949e654b80298580fb2702983314467b1d701db7eeb"} {"question" "q-39765" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(-1,1)"] ["math" "(-1,1)\\cup (1,\\infty )"] ["math" "(-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (-1,1)"] ["math" "(-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (-1,1)\\cup (1,\\infty )"] ["math" "(-\\infty ,1)\\cup (1,\\infty )"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979915/state.json" "file_sha256" "1076857060cce611119c9949e654b80298580fb2702983314467b1d701db7eeb"} {"question" "q-39765" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (-1,1)\\cup (1,\\infty )"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979915/state.json" "file_sha256" "1076857060cce611119c9949e654b80298580fb2702983314467b1d701db7eeb"} {"question" "q-39657" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The interval $I$ consists of all real numbers $x$ such that $0\\le x\\le \\pi .$ What is the largest subset of $I$ on which the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ is continuous?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979915/state.json" "file_sha256" "1076857060cce611119c9949e654b80298580fb2702983314467b1d701db7eeb"} {"question" "q-39657" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Trigonometric functions are continuous at all points in their respective domains.\n\nThe function $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ is continuous over the entire interval $[0,\\pi ],$ but the function $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ is not defined at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nTherefore, the domain of $f(x)$ consists of the union of the intervals $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ],$ namely\n\n$$\n[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cup (\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]\n$$\n\nand the function is also continuous on this union of intervals." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979915/state.json" "file_sha256" "1076857060cce611119c9949e654b80298580fb2702983314467b1d701db7eeb"} {"question" "q-39657" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979915/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "25cc545ce118b199073ded6344ed054eabd863cfb32a7fbe61dfc540a594989a"} {"question" "q-39657" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979915/state.json" "file_sha256" "1076857060cce611119c9949e654b80298580fb2702983314467b1d701db7eeb"} {"question" "q-39657" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cup (\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi )"] ["math" "(0,\\pi )"] ["math" "[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cup (\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi )"] ["math" "[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cup (\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]"] ["math" "[0,\\pi ]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979915/state.json" "file_sha256" "1076857060cce611119c9949e654b80298580fb2702983314467b1d701db7eeb"} {"question" "q-39657" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cup (\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979915/state.json" "file_sha256" "1076857060cce611119c9949e654b80298580fb2702983314467b1d701db7eeb"} {"question" "q-40081" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The interval $I$ consists of all real numbers $x$ such that $-\\pi \\le x\\le \\pi .$ What is the largest subset of $I$ on which the function $g(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)$ is continuous?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979915/state.json" "file_sha256" "1076857060cce611119c9949e654b80298580fb2702983314467b1d701db7eeb"} {"question" "q-40081" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Trigonometric functions are continuous at all points in their respective domains.\n\nThe function $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is continuous over the entire interval $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ but the function $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ is not defined at $x=-\\pi ,0,\\pi .$ \n\nTherefore, the domain of $g(x)$ consists of the union of the intervals $(-\\pi ,0)$ and $(0,\\pi ),$ namely\n\n$$\n(-\\pi ,0)\\cup (0,\\pi )\n$$\n\nand the function is also continuous on this union of intervals." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979915/state.json" "file_sha256" "1076857060cce611119c9949e654b80298580fb2702983314467b1d701db7eeb"} {"question" "q-40081" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979915/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "25cc545ce118b199073ded6344ed054eabd863cfb32a7fbe61dfc540a594989a"} {"question" "q-40081" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979915/state.json" "file_sha256" "1076857060cce611119c9949e654b80298580fb2702983314467b1d701db7eeb"} {"question" "q-40081" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(-\\pi ,0)\\cup (0,\\pi )"] ["math" "(-\\pi ,\\pi )"] ["math" "(0,\\pi ]"] ["math" "[-\\pi ,0)"] ["math" "[-\\pi ,0)\\cup (0,\\pi ]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979915/state.json" "file_sha256" "1076857060cce611119c9949e654b80298580fb2702983314467b1d701db7eeb"} {"question" "q-40081" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(-\\pi ,0)\\cup (0,\\pi )"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979915/state.json" "file_sha256" "1076857060cce611119c9949e654b80298580fb2702983314467b1d701db7eeb"} {"question" "q-302871" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The largest interval on which the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x-8))$ is continuous is\n\n$\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979915/state.json" "file_sha256" "1076857060cce611119c9949e654b80298580fb2702983314467b1d701db7eeb"} {"question" "q-302871" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function is a composition of two functions, a logarithm function, and a cosine function.\n\nThe cosine is continuous at all points in its domain, and its domain consists of all real numbers. So, we focus on the logarithm function.\n\nLikewise, the logarithm function $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x-8)$ is continuous at all points in its domain. To find its domain, we set the expression under the logarithm to be greater than zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-8>0\\,⟹\\,x>4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $f(x)$ is $(4,\\infty )$, and the function is continuous on this interval." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979915/state.json" "file_sha256" "1076857060cce611119c9949e654b80298580fb2702983314467b1d701db7eeb"} {"question" "q-302871" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979915/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "25cc545ce118b199073ded6344ed054eabd863cfb32a7fbe61dfc540a594989a"} {"question" "q-302871" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979915/state.json" "file_sha256" "1076857060cce611119c9949e654b80298580fb2702983314467b1d701db7eeb"} {"question" "q-302871" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979915/state.json" "file_sha256" "1076857060cce611119c9949e654b80298580fb2702983314467b1d701db7eeb"} {"question" "q-302871" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979915/state.json" "file_sha256" "1076857060cce611119c9949e654b80298580fb2702983314467b1d701db7eeb"} {"question" "q-302871" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\infty"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979915/state.json" "file_sha256" "1076857060cce611119c9949e654b80298580fb2702983314467b1d701db7eeb"} {"question" "e-3597" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the largest interval on which the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{4-x}$ is continuous." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979915/example-3597.json" "file_sha256" "3cbf13abcad96bf80bf27ba4b28816c12a43033a21625e61587ca37613a2f292"} {"question" "e-3597" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Our function is a radical function, so it is continuous over all points in its domain.\n\nTo find the domain of $\\sqrt{4-x}$, we set the expression under the radical to be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\n4-x\\geq 0\n\\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad \nx\\leq 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $f(x)$ is $(-\\infty,4]$, and the function is continuous on this interval." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979915/example-3597.json" "file_sha256" "3cbf13abcad96bf80bf27ba4b28816c12a43033a21625e61587ca37613a2f292"} {"question" "e-3598" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the largest union of intervals on which the function $f(x)=\\dfrac{x-3}{x^2-5x+6}$ is continuous." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979915/example-3598.json" "file_sha256" "f24ccae9f08a0a7c6dcb2a2bb72fdadb94a2743a9d3e8b509582ba408d863885"} {"question" "e-3598" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "This is a rational function, so it is continuous at all points in its domain. To find its domain, we start by factoring the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x-3}{{x}^{2}-5x+6} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-3}{(x-2)(x-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x-2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x \\neq 3$.\n\nThis function is continuous everywhere except at the excluded points $x=2$ and $x=3.$\n\nTherefore, the domain consists of the union of the intervals $(-\\infty, 2),$ $(2,3),$ and $(3, \\infty),$ namely\n\n$$\n(-\\infty, 2)\\cup(2,3)\\cup(3, \\infty)\n$$\n\nand the function is continuous on these intervals." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979915/example-3598.json" "file_sha256" "f24ccae9f08a0a7c6dcb2a2bb72fdadb94a2743a9d3e8b509582ba408d863885"} {"question" "e-3599" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the largest interval of continuity for the function $f(x)=\\log (2x-3) + x^2+1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979915/example-3599.json" "file_sha256" "616881e356d6131c47b40303710ac4b09e93598fd295fbc0048b9ee651f6a4a3"} {"question" "e-3599" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function consists of two parts, a logarithm, and a polynomial.\n\nThe polynomial $x^2+1$ is continuous at all points in its domain, and its domain consists of all real numbers. So, we focus on the logarithm.\n\nLikewise, the logarithmic function $\\log(2x-3)$ is continuous at all points in its domain. However, the logarithm is only defined for positive inputs, so to find its domain, we set the expression inside the logarithm to be greater than zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-3 & >0 \\\\ 2x & >3 \\\\ x & >\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)=\\log (2x-3) + x^2 + 1$ is continuous on the interval $\\left(\\dfrac 32,\\infty\\right).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979915/example-3599.json" "file_sha256" "616881e356d6131c47b40303710ac4b09e93598fd295fbc0048b9ee651f6a4a3"} {"question" "e-3600" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" 